- **Parameter names are matched with case.** Each parameter must have the C# name or the XML name of a serialized member. A camelCase parameter such as `start` doesn't match a property named `Start` unless `XmlSerializerOptions.PropertyNameCaseInsensitive` is `true`, which is why the example sets it. System.Text.Json matches without regard to case.
- A parameter that matches no member, and has no default value, makes the serializer throw a `NotSupportedException` the first time the type is used. That includes serializing the type.
- Only one constructor of a type can be marked. Marking two throws a `NotSupportedException`.
- A parameter with no default value is required: XML that lacks it is rejected with an `XmlException`, unless `RespectRequiredConstructorParameters` is `false`.
- Without the attribute, the serializer picks the public constructor with the most parameters among those whose parameters are all named after serialized members. A positional record needs no attribute, because its parameters have the same names as its properties.
- Don't combine constructor-set, get-only properties with `IgnoreReadOnlyProperties`. The option removes those properties from the mapping.
